This is reattached to the current graph in a narrower and safer form: $$\boxed{
\text{LOCAL statement}
\neq
\text{CROSS-FRAME statement}
}$$ and every cross-frame statement must declare whose ruler, tick, and reference wave define the reported coefficient.

The old notebook also required a shared closure event, a declared boundary or comparison point, and a declared counting window before comparing two frames. Those details were originally written using a gate protocol. The current ledger need not require a literal physical gate in every calculation, but the semantic requirement survives: $$\boxed{
\text{same event class}
+
\text{declared reference ownership}
+
\text{declared comparison window}
}$$ are the minimum ingredients of an auditable cross-frame comparison.

The early notebook repeatedly tried to remove the ambiguity produced by phrases such as “360-degree tick.” Its durable idea was: $$\boxed{
\text{FullTurn} := \text{one completed return of a declared cyclic pattern}
}$$ with the further warning that a *Tick* is first an operational closure label and only later receives a calibrated duration.

The old `CUBE-CAPACITY-LOCK-03` is unusually important for the current $g$-factor work because it explicitly stated that its per-tick capacity packet was *not* an energy share and *not* a time share. Its normalized channels were defined through a capacity norm, $$C^2=P^2+I^2,
\qquad
k=\frac{P}{C},
\qquad
R=\frac{I}{C},
\qquad
k^2+R^2=1.$$ The useful root is therefore not “capacity equals energy.” The useful root is the opposite guardrail: $$\boxed{
\text{normalized coefficient}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{energy fraction}
}$$ unless an explicit bridge is supplied.

For a reference frame $F$, let its reference wave carry $$f_F=\frac{1}{\mathrm{Tick}_F},\qquad
\omega_F=2\pi f_F,\qquad
E_F=hf_F=\hbar\omega_F.$$ For any electromagnetic wave or mode described relative to that same reference, $$\boxed{
\frac{E}{E_F}=\frac{f}{f_F}=\frac{\omega}{\omega_F}.
}$$ This relation was already present in the angular-progress branch. Its role in the later g-factor reasoning had simply not been made explicit.

For the zero frame, $$\boxed{\frac{E}{E_0}=\frac{\omega}{\omega_0}.}$$ For the local frame, $$\boxed{\frac{E}{E_L}=\frac{\omega}{\omega_L}.}$$ In the equal-stretch branch already registered in the ledger, $$E_L=\frac{E_0}{D},\qquad \omega_L=\frac{\omega_0}{D},$$ and therefore $$\boxed{
\frac{E_L}{E_0}=\frac{\omega_L}{\omega_0}=\frac{1}{D}.
}$$ Thus the zero-to-local frame conversion rescales the reference energy and the reference angular velocity by the same coefficient.
